WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.677
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:16.923
Unit twenty-six: Social Contract Theory. Today you will analyse legitimacy using were + to infinitive hypotheticals.

4
00:00:17.223 --> 00:00:20.050
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.361
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:26.566
Social contract.

7
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:33.485
Social contract means an imagined agreement through which people accept government in exchange for protection.

8
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:38.033
Social contract theory treats society as a mutual agreement.

9
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:41.651
Your turn. Say: Social contract.

10
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.082
Social contract.

11
00:00:46.817 --> 00:00:47.644
State.

12
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.927
State means the organised political authority of a territory.

13
00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:56.304
Weber defined the state by its monopoly on legitimate force.

14
00:00:56.917 --> 00:00:59.708
Your turn. Say: State.

15
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.312
State.

16
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.063
Consent.

17
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.676
Consent means voluntary agreement to something.

18
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:12.235
Legitimate government rests on the consent of the governed.

19
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.584
Your turn. Say: Consent.

20
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.423
Consent.

21
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:21.103
Rights.

22
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:24.917
Rights means entitlements that others must respect.

23
00:01:25.517 --> 00:01:28.921
Rights protect individuals against arbitrary power.

24
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:32.308
Your turn. Say: Rights.

25
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:36.179
Rights.

26
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:37.999
Authority.

27
00:01:38.983 --> 00:01:42.759
Authority means the recognised power to make decisions and enforce them.

28
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.068
Authority differs from mere power because it claims justification.

29
00:01:47.683 --> 00:01:50.382
Your turn. Say: Authority.

30
00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:54.246
Authority.

31
00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:56.240
State of nature.

32
00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.130
State of nature means a hypothetical condition without government or law.

33
00:02:01.717 --> 00:02:05.365
Hobbes imagined the state of nature as a war of all against all.

34
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.090
Your turn. Say: State of nature.

35
00:02:11.933 --> 00:02:13.306
State of nature.

36
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.132
Obligation.

37
00:02:16.117 --> 00:02:20.136
Obligation means a duty one is morally or legally bound to perform.

38
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.375
Citizens may have an obligation to obey just laws.

39
00:02:24.983 --> 00:02:27.740
Your turn. Say: Obligation.

40
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.798
Obligation.

41
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.690
Legitimacy.

42
00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:37.855
Legitimacy means the widely accepted right to rule.

43
00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.715
Legitimacy fades when rulers ignore the people's consent.

44
00:02:42.317 --> 00:02:45.410
Your turn. Say: Legitimacy.

45
00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:49.384
Legitimacy.

46
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.071
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:54.917 --> 00:02:59.752
Visal, I never signed any contract with the government. Why should I obey its laws?

48
00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:11.284
Good objection. Social contract theorists reply that consent can be tacit. Were people to withdraw consent, authority would collapse.

49
00:03:12.383 --> 00:03:14.464
But we can't realistically withdraw it.

50
00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:22.765
Locke granted that, so he stressed rights. Were a government to violate them, the obligation to obey would weaken.

51
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.664
And without government at all?

52
00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:36.775
Hobbes feared the state of nature. Were the state to disappear, he predicted, life would become solitary, poor, nasty, brutish and short.

53
00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:39.341
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.183 --> 00:03:43.646
Grammar focus: Were plus to infinitive (hypothetical).

55
00:03:43.946 --> 00:03:45.098
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.783 --> 00:03:48.955
Were people to withdraw consent, authority would collapse.

57
00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.591
Were a government to violate rights, the obligation to obey would weaken.

58
00:03:54.483 --> 00:03:57.388
Were the state to disappear, disorder would follow.

59
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:08.857
Were + subject + to + verb = a formal, unlikely or imagined condition (= If people were to withdraw…). The result takes would/could. Negative: Were people not to…

60
00:04:09.617 --> 00:04:14.556
Be careful! Don't say: Were people withdraw consent, authority would collapse.

61
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:20.346
Say: Were people to withdraw consent, authority would collapse.

62
00:04:20.646 --> 00:04:23.040
Were + subject + to + verb.

63
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:26.370
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:28.388
Why Obey?.

65
00:04:28.688 --> 00:04:35.506
Social contract theory seeks to justify political authority by imagining the agreement that free and equal people might make.

66
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:44.734
For Hobbes, life in the state of nature would be a war of all against all, so individuals surrender some freedom to a sovereign in exchange for security.

67
00:04:45.733 --> 00:04:52.946
Locke argued instead that people retain natural rights, and that were a government to violate them, citizens would be entitled to resist.

68
00:04:53.933 --> 00:05:00.600
Rousseau placed legitimacy in the general will, claiming that true freedom lies in obeying laws we prescribe to ourselves.

69
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:08.522
Critics object that no one actually signed the contract, but defenders reply that continued participation signals tacit consent.

70
00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:13.681
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:14.517 --> 00:05:18.142
blank people to withdraw consent, authority would collapse.

72
00:05:18.442 --> 00:05:21.724
A, Were. B, Was. C, If were.

73
00:05:25.267 --> 00:05:27.162
The answer is A: Were.

74
00:05:28.317 --> 00:05:31.524
A hypothetical condition without government is the blank.

75
00:05:31.824 --> 00:05:36.162
A, state of nature. B, general will. C, obligation.

76
00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.048
The answer is A: state of nature.

77
00:05:43.183 --> 00:05:45.659
The widely accepted right to rule is blank.

78
00:05:45.959 --> 00:05:49.659
A, rights. B, legitimacy. C, consent.

79
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.745
The answer is B: legitimacy.

80
00:05:56.883 --> 00:05:58.662
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:58.962 --> 00:06:06.807
A, Were the state to fall, chaos would follow.. B, Were the state fall, chaos would follow.. C, Was the state to fall, chaos follow..

82
00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:14.549
The answer is A: Were the state to fall, chaos would follow..

83
00:06:15.683 --> 00:06:19.448
According to the reading, Locke held that governments that violate rights…

84
00:06:19.748 --> 00:06:24.899
A, must always be obeyed. B, may be resisted. C, are impossible.

85
00:06:28.433 --> 00:06:31.397
The answer is B: may be resisted.

86
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.461
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:36.317 --> 00:06:39.129
Why do people accept the authority of governments?

88
00:06:55.917 --> 00:06:57.475
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:57.775 --> 00:07:22.283
People accept the authority of governments mainly because they gain security, order and services in return. Were people to withdraw their support in large numbers, even a powerful state would struggle to function. Habit and shared values also matter, but legitimacy ultimately depends on fair treatment. When citizens believe that rules are just, obedience is given willingly rather than forced.

90
00:07:22.583 --> 00:07:24.176
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.083 --> 00:07:30.209
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and were plus to infinitive (hypothetical).

92
00:07:30.509 --> 00:07:35.936
Today's value is civic responsibility. Take care of your city, because it is everyone's home.

93
00:07:36.236 --> 00:07:38.981
See you in Unit twenty-seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:39.281 --> 00:07:42.711
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

